Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — the name on a document that’s being used to verify your SSN should match the legal name on your Social Security record (your SSA name). Small differences (using a middle initial, leaving out a middle name, or minor punctuation/spacing differences) are often tolerated for some forms, but for things like tax/benefits/payroll, identity verification, employment I-9s, bank account setup, or official government forms an exact match is safest and may be required.

Practical guidance
- Check the form’s instructions or the organization’s verification guidance first — they often say whether exact legal name is required.  
- If the form is for tax, payroll, unemployment, SSA, background checks, bank account, or immigration-related purposes, use the exact name the SSA has on file (legal name). Mismatch can cause delays, rejected filings, or verification failures.  
- Minor stylistic differences (initial vs full middle name, punctuation) may be acceptable in some cases, but don’t count on it for formal identity checks.  
- If the name on your document is wrong:
  - Ask the issuer how to correct it (some allow a corrected submission or require supporting documents).  
  - If your SSA record is wrong or you’ve legally changed your name, update SSA (Form SS-5 or in-person documentation) so future verifications match.  
  - Provide supporting proof of name change (marriage certificate, court order, passport) if requested.  
- Security: don’t send your full SSN over unsecured email or public chat. If someone asks for it, use a secure portal or call the organization and confirm their procedures. When communicating casually, share only the last four digits if absolutely necessary.

A couple of quick questions so I can help more precisely
- What kind of form is this (employer payroll/I-9, tax form, bank account, DMV, application, etc.)? Requirements differ by issuer.  
- You wrote “XXX-XX-2345 (last four: 6789)” — which last four digits are correct? (You don’t need to repeat the full SSN; only confirm the last four if it matters.)

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short, secure message you can send to the organization asking whether an exact legal-name match is required.  
- Walk you through how to update your SSA record or provide the official SSA guidance links.

