Short answer: usually yes — use the exact name that appears on the identity or records the form is being checked against (most often your Social Security Administration record or the ID you’ll present). A mismatch can cause verification failures or processing delays.

A few practical details:

- Government/tax forms (SSA, IRS, W‑2/1099): the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record exactly. If it doesn’t, the agency may flag it and delay processing.
- Employment/verification (I‑9, background checks): use the name exactly as it appears on the identity document you present (passport, driver’s license, SSN card). If you changed your name, include former names where asked.
- Banks/applications: minor differences (middle initial vs full middle name, punctuation, spacing, or use/omission of a hyphen) are often tolerated, but exact matches reduce friction.
- If you have a legal name change or the name on your SSA record is wrong: update your SSA record before submitting forms that rely on SSN verification. That prevents repeated problems.

I also noticed an inconsistency in the numbers you wrote: you listed "XXX-XX-2345" and then wrote the last four are "6789." You probably shouldn’t post your full SSN here — if you meant to give only the last four, just confirm which four digits are the last four.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short note you can send to the organization explaining the name variation, or
- Walk you through how to check/update your name with the Social Security Administration, or
- Tell you what documentation to provide for a specific form (tell me the form or the organization).

