Thanks — a couple quick points before guidance:
- I won’t repeat your full SSN here. I did notice you wrote two different “last four” values (2345 and 6789). Which one is correct? (No need to share the whole number — just confirm the correct last four.)

General guidance on whether the name on the document must match your SSN record

1. Short answer
- Yes — for most official, tax, and identity-verification purposes the name should match exactly the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches can cause processing delays, rejected tax filings, problems with employment verification (E-Verify/I-9), or banking identity checks.

2. When exact match is most important
- Federal tax forms (W‑2, 1099) and IRS filings.
- Employment verification (I‑9/E‑Verify).
- Applications that explicitly check SSA records.
- Passport, driver’s license, or other government ID processing (these use legal name records).
- Background checks and credit checks.

3. When small differences may be acceptable
- Informal forms, internal company forms, or some non-governmental services may accept minor differences (e.g., including or omitting a middle initial). But acceptance is at the requester’s discretion and may still cause follow-up.

4. Common problem areas
- Married/maiden names or recent legal name changes (if you changed your name but haven’t updated SSA yet).
- Use of nicknames (Bob vs Robert), middle names vs initials, suffixes (Jr., III) — SSA records include these details and they matter.
- Hyphens, spaces, or diacritical marks — SSA records may record them differently; match how it appears on your SSA record.

5. What to do if the names don’t match
- Enter the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card or SSA record when the form requires SSN verification.
- If you’ve legally changed your name and haven’t updated the SSA record, update SSA first (see below).
- If you cannot update SSA immediately, contact the organization receiving the form and tell them the situation — they may accept supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order) or allow you to indicate a “previous name.”
- On forms that provide an “also known as,” “previous names,” or “maiden name” field, include those as needed.

6. How to update your name with Social Security (if needed)
- You typically need: proof of identity (driver’s license, passport), proof of legal name change (marriage certificate, divorce decree, or court order), and proof of citizenship/immigration status if required.
- Visit SSA.gov to find required documents and to make an appointment at your local Social Security office or use the My Social Security online services (if eligible).
- Processing times vary; plan ahead if you need the SSA name update for time-sensitive forms.

7. Practical tips
- Before submitting, check the name on your Social Security card or your online Social Security account.
- Include suffixes exactly as listed (e.g., Jr., Sr., III) if on the SSA record.
- If a form only asks for “name as shown on Social Security card,” use that exact format.
- When possible, redact the SSN except the last four digits if the recipient allows — only provide the full SSN when absolutely necessary and on secure forms.

Would you like help with any of the following?
- I can draft a short message you can send to HR/agency explaining a name mismatch.
- I can draft steps for scheduling an SSA appointment or list the documents you’ll need.
- Or, confirm which last-four digits you meant and I’ll tailor advice to your situation.

