Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card or other government ID unless the form’s instructions explicitly allow a variant.

Details and practical steps
- If the form says “name as shown on Social Security card” or is used for identity verification (tax, payroll, background check, benefits, banking), the name should match exactly (including hyphens, suffixes like Jr./Sr., and the presence/absence of a middle name or initial).
- Some less formal forms (internal surveys, marketing lists) may tolerate small variations (omitting a middle initial, using a common nickname), but that can still cause processing delays or verification flags.
- If your legal name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order), either update your record with the SSA first or include the required legal documents (marriage certificate, court order) with the form so the requester can verify.
- If the form only asks for the last four of your SSN, provide the last four as requested — but confirm in the form instructions whether they need the full SSN instead.

Security and privacy
- Don’t share your full SSN over insecure channels (unsecured email, chat). Use secure upload portals or in-person delivery when possible.
- If someone requests your SSN and you’re unsure why, ask what it’s used for and whether providing just the last four digits or another identifier would suffice.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the agency/HR/office asking whether an exact match to your Social Security card is required (I’ll omit any SSN in the message).
- Give wording for listing name variants or instructions for supplying supporting documents.

Which would you like?

