Short answer: in most cases yes — use the name that exactly matches the identity the organization will check against (usually your name as recorded with the Social Security Administration or on your government ID). Small, obvious formatting differences (periods, middle initial present/absent) sometimes are tolerated, but mismatches can cause delays or require proof.

Details and practical steps
- Why exact matching matters
  - For tax, payroll, benefits, and government forms, agencies and employers match the name you give to their records (often SSA). If the names don’t match, you can get processing delays, rejected filings, or problems with background or identity verification.
  - Banks and other institutions will compare the name you give to the name on your ID. Mismatches can slow account opening or verification.

- What is usually acceptable vs not
  - Usually acceptable: minor punctuation differences (periods, commas), presence or absence of a middle initial, or use of a middle name vs initial — but even these can occasionally trigger a flag.
  - More likely to cause problems: different last name (married vs maiden), different ordering (e.g., surname/compound surname changes), spelling errors, or use of a nickname instead of your legal given name.

- If the form requires an SSN
  - Provide the name that matches the SSA record (or whatever government ID you are using) whenever you are asked to give an SSN. If you give a different name, expect the organization to ask for proof or to request you correct the form.

- If your name on the document doesn’t match SSA or your ID
  - Ask the requester whether they require the “legal name” or the name exactly as appears on your Social Security records or ID.
  - If you must change records, update the SSA (Form SS‑5) or provide a legal-name-change document (marriage certificate, court order, passport) to the requester.
  - If it’s a small typo, ask if you can correct it before submitting.

- Security note
  - Avoid sharing full SSNs over insecure channels. Only provide full SSNs when absolutely required and via a secure method.

