I can help — quick clarification first: you wrote "XXX-XX-2345 (last four: 6789)". Which is correct (the masked SSN or the “last four”)? You don’t need to repeat the full number here — just confirm which last four you mean.

General guidance about name matching on forms
- Best practice: use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card for any form that asks for your SSN. Government agencies and tax/earnings systems match the name on the form to the name associated with your SSN, so spelling differences can cause processing delays or mismatches.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order) but the SSA record hasn’t been updated, either:
  - update your SSA record first (SSA Form SS‑5 / local office) before submitting forms that rely on the SSN, or
  - use the name as it appears on your Social Security card and provide documentation of your name change to the requester.
- For employer/payroll forms (W‑4, onboarding), the employer usually requires the name that matches SSA records to avoid TIN/name mismatch on W‑2s. They may accept your current legal name if you give supporting documents and you’ve updated SSA.
- For banks or private forms, institutions are often more flexible but may still ask for documentation if names differ.
- Minor variations (omitting a middle name or using an initial, a hyphen, or a common nickname) sometimes pass, but there’s no guarantee — use the exact spelling to be safe.
- If a form has a field for “Also known as / Former name,” use it to show the alternate name.

What to do now
1. Check the name on your Social Security card. If it matches the name you want to use, use that exact spelling on the form.  
2. If your legal name has changed and the SSA isn’t updated, either update SSA or include documentation (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license) and/or contact the requester to ask their policy.  
3. If you prefer, tell me: the type of form (IRS/tax, employer onboarding, bank, DMV, etc.) and whether the name on the form differs from your Social Security card. I can suggest exact wording to enter, or draft a short note you can send to the requester explaining the situation.

Privacy note: avoid sharing your full SSN in email or chat. Last-four digits are usually fine for discussion; don’t paste the full number here.

