Length: 2085 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use the exact legal name on your Social Security record (the name shown on your Social Security card) unless the form’s instructions explicitly allow a variation.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exactness matters: many organizations (employers, the IRS, benefits/payments, background checks, banks) verify identity by matching the name + SSN against Social Security Administration (SSA) records. Mismatches can cause delays, rejected applications, tax/benefit reporting problems, or hold-ups in identity verification.
- What “exact” typically includes:
  - First + middle + last name exactly as on your Social Security card. Middle initial vs full middle name can sometimes be tolerated, but matching what SSA has is safest.
  - Hyphens, apostrophes, suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) and changed last names (married, divorced) can matter — include them if they appear on your SSA record.
- When small variations may be OK: some private forms or systems accept common variants (e.g., “Bob” vs “Robert”) or omit punctuation. But you shouldn’t rely on that for government, tax, payroll, or legal forms.
- If your name has changed or the form doesn’t match your SSA record:
  - If you legally changed your name, update your SSA record first (SSA requires proof such as marriage certificate, court order, or passport) or provide the legal-document copies the requester asks for.
  - Contact the organization that issued the form and ask whether they can accept supporting documentation or a name-update process.
  - If they require the SSA name and you can’t update right away, ask what temporary documentation or exception they allow.
- Security reminder: don’t share your full SSN in unsecured messages. Only enter it into trusted, secure forms (look for HTTPS and a known, legitimate requester).

If you want, tell me:
- what form it is (type/issuer) — I can give specific guidance on their typical requirements; or
- whether you’d like help drafting a message to the form issuer explaining a name discrepancy (I can draft it without you posting your full SSN).

