Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: for anything that will be verified against government records (Social Security, taxes, passports, many employer or benefits checks) you should use your full legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card or other government ID. Small differences can cause delays or mismatches.

Details and practical tips:
- When exact matching is required
  - Government forms, payroll/HR, benefits, tax documents, passport/visa paperwork, background checks, and many financial account verifications typically require the legal name that matches the SSA or other government records.
- Minor variations that are often tolerated
  - Middle initial vs full middle name, omission of punctuation (e.g., "St" vs "St."), and small spacing/hyphenation differences sometimes pass—but they can still trigger manual review.
- If your name has changed (marriage, court order, etc.)
  - Use the current legal name that SSA has on file. If you haven’t updated SSA yet, either update your name with the SSA first or include your previous/maiden name where the form asks for “other names used” and attach or be ready to provide supporting documentation (marriage certificate, court order).
- If the form is for an employer or bank
  - Use the name on your SSA record and be prepared to show ID. If the employer needs a different name format (e.g., for display vs legal), they’ll usually ask for the legal name for payroll/tax purposes.
- If you’re unsure
  - Check the form’s instructions or contact the organization directly and ask whether the name must match the SSA record exactly and what documentation they require if it doesn’t.
- Security reminder
  - Only provide your SSN on secure, legitimate sites or forms that require it. Share as little of the number as possible (use masked formats) unless the full SSN is explicitly required for submission.

If you tell me what type of form this is (employer, bank, government agency, DMV, passport, etc.), I can give more specific guidance or draft a short message you can send to the organization to confirm their requirements.

