I won’t repeat your full SSN here. I did notice the masked number and the “last four” you gave don’t match (masked shows “2345” while you wrote last four “6789”) — do you want to confirm which is correct?

Short answer
- In almost all cases where a form asks for your SSN, your name should match your legal name as it appears on the government records that the organization will use to verify the SSN (typically your Social Security record or government ID). Minor variations sometimes are accepted if the organization provides a field for “other name,” “previous name,” or “name used,” but you should follow that form’s instructions.

Details and practical guidance
- Follow the form’s instructions exactly. If it says “Full legal name (as shown on Social Security card)” — use the exact name on your Social Security card (including middle name/initial, hyphens, Jr./Sr. suffixes as applicable).
- Government agencies (SSA, IRS, passport office, DMV): These commonly require an exact/legal match. For example, tax refunds or processing can be delayed if your name does not match SSA records.
- Banks and employers: Usually require the name that matches your ID and SSN record; they may accept minor differences if you provide supporting ID or documentation.
- Minor variations that may be acceptable: using a middle initial vs full middle name (sometimes), presence/absence of a hyphen or diacritics (depends on the organization), or including/excluding a suffix — but don’t assume; check the form’s guidance.
- If your legal name has changed but the SSA/records are not updated: update SSA/other records first if possible. If you must submit the form now, include supporting documentation (marriage certificate, court order) or an explanatory note and follow the organization’s guidance.

If you find a mismatch while filling the form
1. Look for fields like “Also known as / Previous name” and fill them if available.
2. Attach or be ready to provide a copy of the ID that matches the SSN record.
3. Check the form’s FAQ or contact the organization’s support/helpline — ask whether the form requires an exact match to SSA records.
4. If necessary, correct the underlying record (e.g., SSA) before submitting critical government forms.

Privacy tip
- Only transmit your SSN through secure channels the organization requests. Avoid emailing full SSN over unencrypted email unless the recipient explicitly instructs you to and you trust their process. Use masked SSN (last 4 digits) when possible.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether the name must match SSA records (I’ll avoid including your full SSN in the draft), or
- Help find the exact instruction on a specific form if you tell me which form/agency it is?

