I can help. First: I will not send or store your SSN or other PII to any external address or vault.

Short answer
- If the form is asking for identity that will be checked against government records (tax, Social Security, passport, driver’s license), you should use your full legal name exactly as it appears on that government record. Mismatches can cause delays or rejections.
- For less formal uses (internal forms, mailing lists), small differences (middle initial present/absent, a hyphen, or common spelling variants) are often accepted, but check the form’s instructions.

Guidance by situation
- Tax / payroll / W‑2: The name should match the Social Security Administration’s record (your name on file with SSA). If it doesn’t, your employer’s reporting may not match SSA and that can create tax/verification problems.
- Passport: Must match your passport exactly for travel or any passport-related processing.
- Driver’s license / state ID: Use the name on the ID unless the form gives an “other names/aliases” field.
- Legal documents (court, deeds): Use your legal name as shown on the relevant certificate or court order.
- Banking / financial institutions: Usually require the legal name that matches ID and tax records.

If your name changed or doesn’t match SSA
- If your SSA record is out of date, update it with SSA (bring required documents such as marriage certificate, court order, or birth certificate). After SSA updates, provide corrected info to employers or agencies.
- Many forms include an “also known as / former name / alias” field — use that and attach supporting documentation if available.

Practical steps you can take now
1. Check the form’s instructions or any “notes”/help text on the page. It will often say whether legal name is required.
2. If the form will be matched to government/tax records, use the name on your Social Security record or update SSA first.
3. If you’re unsure, contact the entity that issued the form (HR, agency, bank) and ask whether an exact match is required or whether you can indicate an alias.
4. If you want, I can draft a short message you can send to the agency/employer explaining your situation or help find SSA contact/update instructions.

If you want me to help further, tell me which specific form or agency this is (e.g., employer payroll form, passport application, bank account) and whether the name on your Social Security card differs from the one on the form. I’ll draft a suggested message or next steps.

