I can help — first a quick privacy note: I won’t store or transmit your full SSN and I recommend avoiding posting it in messages. Also I noticed the SSN you typed has two different last-4 values (one in the masked pattern and a different one in parentheses). Which last-four did you mean?

General guidance about name spelling on forms
- Best practice: use the exact name that appears on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Many government and payroll systems match name + SSN against SSA records; a mismatch can cause processing delays, tax/reporting problems, or require corrections.
- Government forms (SSA, passport applications, immigration, tax reporting): the name must match legal/SSA records. If your legal name changed (marriage, court order) you should update SSA before submitting forms that will be matched to your SSN.
- Employer/payroll and IRS forms (W‑2, 1099): employers report using the name on file with SSA. If it doesn’t match, the IRS may send notices or delay refunds.
- Banks and private companies: slightly more tolerant of minor variations (using a middle initial, abbreviated middle name), but major differences or different last names can trigger additional identity verification.
- IDs (driver’s license, passport): those should show your current legal name exactly; those are the primary identity documents.

What to do if your name doesn’t match the SSA record
1. Confirm which name is on your Social Security card. You can view or request info via your mySocialSecurity account or contact SSA.
2. If the SSA record is wrong or outdated, update it (Form SS‑5 and required proof: marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate, etc.). Some updates can be started online or require a local office visit.
3. When filling a form now:
   - If possible, enter the name exactly as on your SSA card.
   - If the form has fields for “Also known as / Former name,” include your other name(s).
   - If you must submit now and the name differs, attach a short note or contact the recipient to explain that you are updating SSA and will provide corrected info (or provide supporting documents).
4. If you expect tax/employment reporting, notify payroll/HR so they can correct their records before reports are filed.

Need help with any of these next steps?
- I can draft a short message to HR/Payroll or to the agency explaining the name/SSN situation.
- If you tell me what type of form you’re filling out, I’ll give specific wording for the form or a suggested note to include.

