Short answer: for any official use of your SSN (SSA records, IRS/tax forms, employer verification, E‑Verify, background checks, passports, etc.) your name should match the name on your Social Security record (your SSN card) as exactly as possible — including spelling, punctuation, middle name/initial, and suffixes. Mismatches can cause verification failures, delays, or returned/incorrect processing.

Why it matters
- Agencies and employers electronically match name + SSN against SSA/IRS databases. Even small differences (missing/mistyped letters, swapped middle name vs. initial, missing hyphen or suffix) can trigger a mismatch.  
- A mismatch can delay hiring, tax filings, benefits, or background checks and may require extra documentation to clear.

When small differences are usually acceptable
- Informal internal forms (non‑legal surveys, some account nicknames) often allow variations.  
- Some organizations will accept a common short form (e.g., “Mike” vs “Michael”) if you also provide ID, but you should not assume that.

What you should do
1. Check the name on your SSN card and on primary ID (driver’s license, passport). That is the name agencies will expect.  
2. If the form is official and requests SSN, use the exact legal name on your SSN card.  
3. If your name has changed (marriage, court order), update your SSA record before submitting SSN‑dependent official forms, or ask the recipient how to submit proof of the name change.  
4. If a form shows a typo, correct it before submitting. Don’t submit with a known error.  
5. If you cannot change the form before submission, include supporting ID or a brief notarized affidavit if requested, and contact the recipient for instructions.  
6. For tax filings, verify your name/SSN with SSA now to avoid IRS mismatch notices later.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email you can send to the organization/agency asking whether they require an exact match and what proof they accept.  
- Help you word the name on the form (you can paste the non‑sensitive parts — do not paste full SSN).

Note: please avoid posting your full SSN here. You already included the last four — that’s fine for discussion — but don’t share the full number in public text.

