I won’t repeat or transmit your SSN (and I can’t send it anywhere). That said, here’s practical guidance.

Short answer
- In most cases, yes — the name on the form should match your legal name as shown on the ID or Social Security record used to verify your SSN. Mismatches (even small ones) can cause verification failures or processing delays.

Why it matters
- Agencies that match SSN to identity records (Social Security Administration, IRS, banks, background-check vendors, employers) compare the name and SSN on the form to their records. If they don’t match, the record can be flagged and the transaction may be delayed, rejected, or require extra identity documents.
- Minor differences (middle initial vs. full middle name, punctuation, or accent marks) are sometimes tolerated, but automated systems are sensitive and may still fail.

What to do, by situation
- Social Security / SSA matters: Use the exact name on your Social Security card. If that’s outdated, update SSA first.
- Taxes / IRS (filing, W‑2s): Name must match IRS/SSA records. A mismatch can cause e-file rejections or refund delays.
- Employer / Payroll: Provide the name that matches your SSN/SSA record and the ID you’ll present for Form I-9.
- Banks / Loans / Background checks: Use your legal name; if you regularly go by a different name, tell the institution and provide supporting documentation.
- DMV / passport / government IDs: Use the legal name used on supporting documents.

If you’ve already submitted a form with a mismatch
- Contact the recipient immediately, explain there may be a name/SSN mismatch, and ask how to correct it.
- Be prepared to provide proof (Social Security card, birth certificate, marriage certificate, court order) — submit via their secure channel only.

Security and transmission
- Never send your full SSN over unencrypted email or public chat. Ask the recipient for a secure upload portal, in-person submission, fax to a secure line, or a verified phone procedure.
- If asked to provide the SSN in an email, ask for an alternative secure method or whether you can provide just the last four digits initially.

If you want, I can:
- Tell you what to put on the form if you tell me the form’s purpose (employer, tax, bank, government).
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking what name format they require and their secure method for receiving SSNs (I won’t include your SSN in the draft).

Which form is this for? I can give more specific advice and draft a secure request message.

