Short answer: for official use (tax, payroll, government IDs, benefits, background checks) your name should match exactly the name on your Social Security Administration (SSA) record. For less formal uses (shipping, newsletters, some account signups) small variations are usually fine — but when the SSN is involved, exact match is safest.

A few practical points and next steps:

- I noticed a possible inconsistency in what you typed (you wrote "XXX-XX-2345" and also "last four: 6789"). If you intend to rely on SSA verification, be sure you know the correct SSN last four before submitting anything. Don’t post the full SSN in insecure places or public messages.

- Why exact matching matters: employers, the IRS, banks, and many government agencies verify the SSN against the SSA name. Mismatched names can cause delays, rejected forms, payroll or tax-reporting problems, or notices from the IRS/SSA.

- What counts as a mismatch:
  - Omitted or different middle name/initials, misspellings, different punctuation (hyphens, accents), and missing suffixes (Jr./III) can all cause verification problems.
  - Minor formatting differences (e.g., "Robert" vs. "Rob") often trigger verification failures if the SSA record uses the legal name.

- If your name on the form doesn’t match your SSA record:
  - Use the exact legal name on your SSA card if possible.
  - If your legal name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order), update your SSA record first or provide the agency with the legal-change documentation they request (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).
  - Contact the form issuer (employer/agency/bank) and ask whether a slight variation is acceptable or whether they require the SSA-legal name and documentation.

- How to check or update your SSA record:
  - You can verify your name and SSN through your SSA account (mySocialSecurity at ssa.gov) or by contacting the SSA office.
  - To change your name with SSA you generally need identity and proof-of-name-change documents (e.g., marriage certificate, passport, court order). Check ssa.gov for current requirements.

- Privacy/security tip: Only share SSN (or full SSN) through secure, trusted channels and when absolutely necessary. Don’t email or post it publicly.

