Short answer: for most official identity checks (tax, government, banking), the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security record (your Social Security card/legal name). Small formatting differences (periods, extra spaces) are often normalized, but differences in spelling, omitted middle names or suffixes, or a changed last name can cause verification failures.

Guidance and next steps
- Check the form wording first — it may say “legal name,” “name as shown on Social Security card,” or “current name.” Follow that instruction.
- If the agency will electronically verify the SSN against SSA records (common for payroll/tax/benefits), use the exact name the SSA has on file. If your name has changed (marriage, etc.), you’ll need to update SSA or provide previous names as requested.
- Middle names/initials and punctuation: usually okay, but including or excluding a middle name/initial can sometimes trigger a mismatch. Suffixes (Jr., III) and hyphenated names matter.
- If you’re unsure, contact the form issuer and ask whether they require an exact SSA match or whether they accept an explanation/alternate name. That avoids delays.
- If you get a rejection, be prepared to show proof (SSN card, updated ID, marriage certificate, court order) or to request that the organization accept previous names.
- Security note: never share your full SSN over insecure channels (public chat, unencrypted email). Only provide SSN where explicitly required and to trusted, secure recipients.

