Short answer: if the form is tied to your Social Security Number (tax reporting, benefits, payroll, E‑Verify, etc.), your name should match what the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file. Minor differences (middle initial, hyphenation, use of a maiden or married name) sometimes are tolerated, but mismatches can trigger delays, mismatches in tax records, or E‑Verify issues.

What to watch for
- Required exact match:
  - Tax documents (W‑2, 1099) should use the name on your SSA record so the IRS and SSA match.
  - SSA benefits and many government forms require the legal name on file.
  - E‑Verify compares the name + SSN to SSA records; differences can cause a tentative non‑confirmation.
- Sometimes okay:
  - Nonofficial forms (membership, marketing) often accept small variations, but check the form instructions.
  - If a form includes an “also known as,” “previous names,” or “maiden name” field, fill that in.

If your name doesn’t match SSA records
- Use the exact name on your SSA record for any form that will be compared to your SSN, or
- Contact the agency/employer requesting the form and ask what they’ll accept (they may allow an “aka” field or supporting documents), or
- Update your SSA record so it matches your current legal name.

How to update SSA (if needed)
- Fill out Form SS‑5 (Application for Social Security Card) at ssa.gov (see ssa.gov/ssnumber or search “Form SS‑5”).
- Provide proof of identity (U.S. passport or state driver’s license) and proof of name change (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).
- You can mail original/certified documents or visit your local SSA office. SSA phone: 1‑800‑772‑1213 (TTY 1‑800‑325‑0778).

Privacy tip
- Avoid sending your full SSN by email. Provide just the last four digits when possible and only to trusted, secure recipients.

Would you like help with any of the following?
- I can draft a short message you can send to the agency or employer asking whether they require the SSA name exactly.
- I can draft the SSA Form SS‑5 information or a checklist of documents to bring to the SSA office.

