Short answer: usually yes — use the exact legal name that’s on file with the Social Security Administration (the name on your Social Security card) when a form asks for your SSN. A mismatch can cause verification failures, processing delays, or rejected forms.

Details and practical guidance:
- When they ask for “name as on Social Security card” or for identification used for tax, payroll, benefits, banking, or government purposes, enter the exact legal name on your SSN/SSA record (including middle name/initial if that’s on the card).
- Small differences (an omitted middle initial, punctuation, or diacritics) sometimes pass manual review, but automated checks are strict and can fail on seemingly minor mismatches. If you want to be safe, match the SSA record exactly.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order, etc.), either:
  - Use the new legal name if you’ve updated SSA records, or
  - Use the name on your SSA record and provide documentation of the name change (marriage certificate, court order) if requested.
- If you routinely go by a nickname or “also known as,” look for a field for former/other names or add that information as instructed.
- If you’ve already submitted a form with a misspelling, contact the requester right away, explain the correction, and be prepared to provide ID or documentation they require.

Security and privacy:
- Never send your full SSN over unencrypted email, text, or to unknown/unverified sites. Only give the full SSN when it is strictly required and the recipient is legitimate and secure. If possible, provide only the last four digits.
- Check that any online form is secure (HTTPS), that the requester is legitimate, and that there’s a clear reason for needing your SSN.
- If you’re unsure whether the form really needs the full SSN, ask the organization why it’s required and whether the last four digits will be sufficient.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether they require the exact legal name or only the last four SSN digits, and whether there’s a secure way to submit the full SSN.
- Help find the SSA contact or walk through how to check the name on your Social Security record.

