Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — whenever a form is asking for your SSN or is an official/government/financial document, your name should match your legal name exactly (the name on your Social Security card or other government ID). Small differences (missing middle initial, punctuation, accent marks) sometimes go through, but mismatches can delay processing or cause the form to be rejected.

Details and practical steps
- Why exact matching matters
  - Government and tax forms (SSA, IRS), payroll, banks, and background checks usually verify your name against government records. A mismatch can block processing or trigger manual review.
- Common acceptable vs. problematic differences
  - Often acceptable: presence/absence of a middle initial, minor punctuation differences (e.g., "O'Neil" vs "Oneil") — but not guaranteed.
  - Problematic: different legal last name (maiden vs. married), transposed names, omitted suffixes (Jr./Sr.), or significant spelling differences.
- If you haven’t submitted yet
  - Enter the exact name shown on your Social Security card or passport.
  - If your legal name has changed and you haven’t updated SSA records, update them first (or include documentation as requested).
- If you already submitted with a mismatch
  - Contact the organization that received the form and ask how to correct it.
  - Be prepared to provide a copy of your government ID or Social Security card (send by secure method only).
  - Ask whether they accept a scanned copy via a secure portal or require an in-person visit.
- Protecting your SSN
  - Avoid sending the full SSN by email. Only share the last four digits when possible, and use secure channels when the full SSN is required.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email or phone script to request a name-correction from the organization (I’ll avoid including your full SSN).
- Look at the exact wording of the form (paste it here but please redact the SSN) and suggest the best entry.

Which of those would you like me to do?

