Short answer: for any form tied to your SSN (tax, government benefits, payroll, official ID checks) your name should match the name on the Social Security Administration (SSA) record or on the legal ID you will present. For less formal forms, small differences may be acceptable, but it depends on the organization.

Guidance and practical steps
- Why exactness matters: agencies that verify your SSN will compare the name you give to the name on SSA records. A mismatch can cause delays, rejected filings, or additional identity verification.
- What “match” typically means: full legal name, including middle name/initial and suffixes (Jr./Sr./III) when those appear on SSA records or your ID. Punctuation (hyphens, periods) is often ignored, but spelling differences and omitted middle names can trigger mismatches.
- Common acceptable variations: dropping a middle initial is sometimes fine for routine forms; using hyphenated vs. combined surname may be OK for some organizations. For anything tied to the SSN or taxes, assume stricter requirements.
- If you think the SSA record is wrong or out of date: contact the Social Security Administration to check and, if necessary, correct your name on file. That fixes downstream verification issues.
- If the form allows extra fields: use fields for “previous/maiden name” or “also known as (aka)” where available to note alternate spellings/names.
- If you’re unsure: ask the organization that issued the form (HR, bank, agency) whether the variant you plan to use is acceptable — they can tell you whether they require the exact SSA/legal-name match.
- Security note: don’t send your full SSN or other sensitive details over unsecured email. If they request the SSN, provide it only through a secure form, in person, or by a method they specify as secure.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether the name must match SSA/legal ID exactly, or
