Guidance and concrete steps
- Check the form’s instructions or the agency’s website first — they’ll often say “use your full legal name as shown on your Social Security card” or similar.
- For tax forms (IRS): the name on the return must match the name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA) or your return can be delayed.
- For passports and IDs: use your legal name shown on supporting documents; name changes often require documentation.
- For employment verification (I-9, background checks): present the required original documents; names on those documents must be consistent with what you enter.
- If your name has recently changed (marriage, divorce, court order):
  - Either update your Social Security record before submitting forms that will be cross-checked, or
  - Provide documentation of the change (marriage certificate, court order) as instructed by the agency.
- Minor variations (middle initial vs full middle name, punctuation, accents) sometimes are accepted, but they can still trigger manual review. When possible, match exactly to the legal document the organization will use for verification.

Security note
- Don’t email your full SSN. If the form asks for an SSN, use a secure portal or the form’s secure method. Sharing only the last four digits is safer for routine verification.

If you want, I can:
- Look up the form’s specific instructions if you tell me which form/agency it is, or
- Draft a short message for you to send to the agency/HR asking whether they require the exact legal name and how to submit supporting documentation.

