Short answer: preferably yes — the name on the form should match your legal name as it appears on your Social Security record (the name on your SSN card). Many agencies and employers use an exact-match check and mismatched names can delay processing.

Details and practical guidance
- Why exactness matters: payroll, tax reporting (W-2/1099), benefits, background checks and many government systems match names against SSA records. If the name differs they may request proof or reject the submission.
- Small variations often tolerated: missing/extra punctuation, accents, or a missing middle initial are frequently accepted. Policies vary by organization.
- If your name has legally changed: update your name with the Social Security Administration first (you’ll need documents like a marriage certificate, court order, or updated birth certificate). Once SSA record is updated, use that updated name on forms.
- If you can’t update SSA before submitting: include a copy of supporting ID (SSN card, driver’s license) and add a short note or checkbox if the form has an “also known as” / “other names” field. If the receiving organization still objects, contact their helpdesk and explain you’re submitting supporting documentation.
- For sensitive submissions/online forms: use the organization’s secure portal; never email your full SSN. If the form only asks for “last four digits,” supply just those digits (and avoid posting the full SSN in insecure channels).

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short explanation you can attach to the form (e.g., “My legal name on file with SSA is X — enclosed proof”), or
- Help you find the SSA name-change instructions or the specific agency’s requirements.

Tell me what the form is for (employer, tax, passport, bank, etc.) and I’ll give tailored wording and next steps.

