Short answer: for official uses (Social Security, IRS/tax forms, payroll/employment, many government benefits) your name should match the exact name on your Social Security card or the agency’s required legal name. For informal forms it’s usually less strict, but when an organization is matching your name to your SSN, exact/legal spelling matters.

Details and practical steps
- Why exact matching matters: agencies and employers compare the name you give to the name on SSA/IRS records. Mismatches (even small ones) can cause verification failures, tax processing delays, payroll problems, or requests for documentation.
- What usually is required:
  - Social Security and IRS-related forms: your legal name as shown on your Social Security card (or the name you’ve reported to SSA).
  - Employment/payroll (including W‑2): use the name that matches SSA records so earnings are credited correctly.
  - Banks and some government forms: use the name that matches your ID and the SSN on file.
- Which variations are often okay:
  - Middle initials vs. full middle name, punctuation, and capitalization are often tolerated.
  - Nicknames, reversed first/last name order, omitted suffixes (Jr./Sr.) or different last-name spellings can cause problems.
  - Hyphenation or diacritics sometimes matter depending on the system.
- If your name on the form doesn’t match SSA records:
  - If possible, correct the form to match your Social Security card.
  - If your legal name has changed and you haven’t updated SSA, consider updating your SSA record first (then use the updated name).
  - If you already submitted it, contact the organization’s HR or the agency to ask how to correct it and what documentation they need (copy of SS card, government ID, marriage/court documents for name change).
- Documents commonly required to change SSA name: proof of identity (passport or driver’s license), proof of citizenship or lawful status, and proof of the name change (marriage certificate, court order).
- Safety reminder: avoid posting or sharing your full SSN publicly. Sharing the last four digits is common for verification, but keep the full number private unless required and on a secure channel.

If you tell me what type of form this is (tax, employment, bank, application for benefits, etc.), I can give specific wording to enter or draft a short message you can send to the agency/HR asking how to proceed.

