Short answer: yes — in most cases the name on the document should match the legal name on file with the agency that will verify your SSN (typically the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches can cause processing delays, identity‑matching problems, or requests for additional proof.

Guidance and practical steps
- Check the form’s instructions first. Many forms explicitly say “enter the name as shown on your Social Security card” or “enter your legal name.”
- For government/tax uses (SSA, IRS forms like W‑9 or W‑2): use the exact legal name that SSA has on record. If those don’t match, the IRS or payer may reject or delay processing.
- For employment and ID verification (I‑9, background checks, passport, driver’s license): use your legal name; if it’s different from what’s on file, you may need to provide supporting documents (SS card, marriage certificate, court order).
- Minor formatting differences (periods, commas, hyphens, middle initial vs full middle name) are sometimes tolerated, but it’s safest to match punctuation and spacing exactly when possible.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order): either provide your current legal name and indicate former name(s) where asked, or update your Social Security record before submitting the form to avoid mismatches.
- If you’re unsure, contact the organization requesting the form or SSA directly to confirm how they want the name entered. For SSA info, see ssa.gov or call their official number — avoid sending SSNs over unsecured channels.

Security note
- Don’t send your full SSN over email or chat unless you’re using a secure, trusted channel the recipient specified. If you must provide an SSN, prefer secure upload portals, encrypted messages, or in‑person submission.
