--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 1801 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — for official forms tied to your Social Security record, use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security card (or the name you’ve legally changed to). Minor formatting differences (middle initial, punctuation, hyphens) are sometimes tolerated, but exact legal-name mismatches can cause processing delays or rejections.

What to do, step-by-step:
- Check the form’s instructions first. Many agencies and employers explicitly say to use your “legal name” or “name on Social Security card.” Follow that guidance.
- If the form is being used to verify your SSN (tax, payroll, benefits, SSA matching), make the name match your SSA record exactly. A mismatch can trigger an SSA/name-SSN mismatch and delay processing.
- For other uses (bank accounts, subscriptions, non-government forms) small differences (using a middle initial vs full middle name, or including/removing Jr./Sr.) may be acceptable — but organizations decide case-by-case and may ask for ID or documentation.
- If your name on the document is wrong:
  - Correct it before submitting if you can.
  - If you can’t change it, attach or be ready to provide supporting documentation (Social Security card, driver’s license, marriage certificate, court order) to show the legal name.
- If your Social Security record itself is out of date, contact the Social Security Administration to update it (you typically need proof of the legal name change).

Privacy note: don’t send your full SSN in email or public messages. Only provide it through secure channels when required, and mask it where possible (e.g., show only the last four digits).

If you tell me which form or agency this is (for example: SSA, IRS W‑4, employer onboarding, bank account opening), I can give more specific guidance on that case.

