Assistant: Short answer: usually yes — use your legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card) unless the form specifically allows a variation. Small differences sometimes pass verification, but mismatches frequently cause delays or verification failures.

What to expect and when exact matching matters
- Payroll, tax reporting, Social Security benefits, unemployment, and most government forms: the name should match SSA records. A mismatch can stop payroll, cause incorrect tax reporting (W‑2s/1099s), or delay benefits.
- Bank account opening, background checks, and identity verification: organizations often require the legal name on your official ID (driver’s license, passport) and may check it against your SSN.
- Informal forms or internal notes: usually more flexible about nicknames or initials.

If your name on the form doesn’t match your SSA record
- If the form requires the SSA/legal name but you use a different name (married name, preferred name, missing middle name, etc.), either:
  - Put the legal name on the form (best), or
  - Contact the organization first and ask whether they accept an alternate form of your name and what documentation they need.
- If your SSA record is out of date (e.g., you changed your name and haven’t updated SSA), update your Social Security record before or at the same time you submit important forms. To update SSA you typically need proof of identity and proof of the name change (passport, driver’s license, birth certificate, marriage certificate, or court order).

Documents commonly accepted as proof
- Government photo ID (passport or driver’s license)
- Social Security card
- Marriage certificate or court order for legal name change
- Birth certificate (for original name)

Security and privacy tip
- Only provide your full SSN when absolutely required and only through a secure, trusted channel. Use redaction (last four digits) when possible, and avoid sending the full SSN in unsecured messages.

If you want, tell me:
- Which specific form or agency this is for (employer, bank, government form, etc.), and I’ll give tailored advice or draft a short message you can send asking what they require.

