Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — for most official forms you should use your exact legal name as it appears on your Social Security card (or other legal ID). Using a different spelling or format can cause delays, mismatches, or requests for proof.

Details and practical guidance
- When exact matching is required
  - Federal/tax forms (IRS returns, W‑2s): the name should match SSA records. A mismatch can trigger processing delays or an SSN/name mismatch notice from the IRS.
  - Employment verification (I-9) and background checks: use your legal name exactly as on ID documents.
  - Banks, passport, DMV and other government IDs: they require the legal name on supporting documents.
- When small differences may be acceptable
  - Informal forms (some memberships, newsletters) often accept variations (use of a middle initial vs full middle name, nicknames) — but don’t assume this for anything that verifies identity or SSN.
- If the name on the form doesn’t match your SSA/ID record
  - Correct the form so it matches your legal name, or
  - If the form has a space for “also known as / previous name,” include the alternate, and attach or offer supporting documentation if requested, or
  - Contact the organization asking for the form and ask whether they require the exact SSA spelling or will accept a corrected/annotated version.
- If your legal name has changed
  - Update your Social Security record (Form SS‑5 with required proof such as marriage certificate or court order). Many downstream issues go away once SSA and your IDs are consistent.
- Security and privacy
  - Never send your full SSN over unencrypted email or post it publicly. Use secure portals, encrypted email, or give it in person/over the phone to verified recipients only.

If you tell me what type of form this is (tax, employment, bank, DMV, etc.), I can give a more specific recommendation and — if you like — draft a short note you can send to the agency asking whether they require the exact SSA spelling.

