Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name that matches your Social Security Administration (SSA) records (the name on your SSN card) whenever a form asks for your SSN. Mismatches can cause verification failures, delays, or requests for additional documentation.

Details and practical steps:
- What to match: ideally the full legal name on file with the SSA (first, middle if listed, last, and any suffix like Jr./III). If the form is verifying your SSN, the name must match SSA records.
- Minor variations: small formatting differences (periods, commas, extra spaces) are usually tolerated. Abbreviations (Bill vs. William), omitted/added middle names or initials, or a different last name (maiden vs. married) often trigger manual review or rejection.
- If your name has changed: before submitting SSN information, update your SSA record (Form SS-5 or visit your local SSA office) or be prepared to provide legal proof (marriage certificate, court order, or other documentation) to the form recipient.
- If the form asks for the name as on your ID (driver’s license) rather than SSA, make sure the ID name and SSA name match; if they don’t, update whichever record is out of date.
- If you’re unsure what the form issuer requires: contact them and ask whether they need the name shown on your SSN card or the name on your government ID — and what documents they accept to resolve discrepancies.

Security note: don’t post your full SSN in emails or online chats. It’s fine to use the last four digits when necessary.

If you want, tell me:
- what name appears on the form vs. your legal/SSA name (don’t include your full SSN), and I can advise whether that difference is likely to be acceptable or help you draft a short message to the form issuer requesting guidance.

