Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — for official forms that will be verified against government or payroll records, your name should match the legal name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA) or the ID the organization will use to verify you. Small, non‑substantive differences (use/omission of a middle initial, punctuation, accents) are often tolerated, but spelling differences, omitted or incorrect last names, or missing suffixes (Jr./III) can cause verification failures or delays.

Practical guidance
- Follow the form’s instructions first. Many forms explicitly say “enter your name exactly as shown on your Social Security card” — follow that.
- If the form will be used for taxes, payroll, benefits, background checks, or government services: enter the exact legal name on your Social Security card or other government ID.
- Middle name/initials and suffixes: include them if they appear on your SS card (or include the suffix if required). Using an initial instead of the full middle name is often fine, but matching the SSA record is safest.
- Hyphens, apostrophes, and diacritics (accents): usually not critical, but try to match the official spelling. Some systems strip punctuation/accents, but you shouldn’t change letters.
- If your name changed (marriage, court order) and SSA hasn’t been updated: either update your SSA record first or attach/submit the supporting documentation the form requests (marriage certificate, court order, driver’s license).
- If there’s a separate field for “other names/previous names/maiden name,” fill that in too — that helps verifiers find the correct record.
- If you’re unsure, contact the agency or organization receiving the form and ask how strict their name-matching is and what to do if your legal name has changed.

Security note
- Avoid sending your full SSN over insecure channels (email, chat) unless the recipient specifically requires it and you’re sure the channel is secure. If a form asks for the SSN, prefer entering it directly on the secure form rather than emailing it.

If you tell me which form or which organization is asking for the SSN, I can give more specific advice or draft the exact name format you should use.

