Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: For official forms you should generally use your legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card and other government IDs. Mismatched spellings or different name formats can cause delays, rejected forms, or extra verification steps.

Details and practical guidance
- Social Security Administration (SSA): Use the name on your SSA record (your Social Security card). If the name on the form doesn’t match SSA’s records, update your SSA record (Form SS-5) before or alongside submitting the form.
- IRS / tax returns: The name you use on tax returns should match the name tied to your SSN/SSA record. A mismatch may trigger an IRS name/SSN mismatch and delay processing or refunds.
- Employment / I-9 & background checks: Employers must verify identity with a government ID. Use the name exactly as shown on the document you present (driver’s license, passport, SSN card).
- Banks, mortgage, loan, notarized documents: These typically require the legal name on your ID. Hyphens, suffixes (Jr., III), or middle names/initials should match or be consistent with your ID.
- Minor variations: Middle initial vs full middle name is often accepted, but nicknames (e.g., “Mike” vs “Michael”) and omitted hyphens/diacritics sometimes cause problems. When in doubt, use the exact legal spelling and format.

If your name doesn’t match
- If the discrepancy is small (middle initial vs full name) check the form instructions — some forms accept minor variations.
- If it’s a legal-name change (marriage, divorce, court order), update the relevant government records (SSA, DMV, passport) and provide supporting documents as requested.
- If you can’t change SSA records immediately, contact the agency asking what documentation they’ll accept (some will accept a copy of the name-change document plus ID).
- If a form allows an “also known as / formerly known as” field, use it.

Security note
- Avoid sharing your full SSN except on secure, authorized forms. Providing just the last four digits is safer when full SSN isn’t required.

Would you like help with any of the following?
- Tell me which specific form or agency this is (name of form or organization) and I’ll give tailored guidance.
- I can draft a short message you can send to the agency asking whether they require the exact SSA spelling.
- I can outline the documents you’d need to update your SSA record if you want to change the name on file.

