I can help — but first: I will not store, send, or share your SSN or any other sensitive number. Don’t post the full SSN in messages; if you need me to refer to it, say “last four” or confirm you’re comfortable sharing it for drafting a private message.

Short answer
- When a form asks for your name, it usually needs the legal name that matches the Social Security Administration (SSA) records or the identity document the organization will use to verify you. If the form specifically asks for the “name on your Social Security card,” use that exact spelling/format. If it’s a generic “full name” field, use your current legal name (which may be the same as or different from the name on the SSA card).

Why it matters
- Government and tax agencies (SSA, IRS) and many employers/banks verify name+SSN. A mismatch can cause delays, rejected forms, incorrect tax filings, or verification failures.
- Minor differences (middle initial omitted, punctuation, accent marks) are sometimes tolerated, but exact matches reduce risk.

Practical steps
1. Check the form’s instructions
   - If it explicitly says “name as on Social Security card,” use that exact name.
   - If it asks for “legal name” or “current full name,” use your current legal name.

2. If your name on the form doesn’t match SSA records
   - Consider updating the SSA record to your current legal name (if your name has changed). SSA uses Form SS‑5 — you’ll need proof (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate, etc.). Visit ssa.gov or your local SSA office.
   - Alternatively, contact the organization receiving the form to ask whether they accept a difference and what documentation they require (e.g., marriage certificate or court order).

3. For employers / payroll / taxes
   - Make sure the name on your W‑4/W‑2 matches SSA records to avoid IRS name/SSN mismatch notices.

4. For IDs (driver’s license, passport)
   - If you plan to present an ID for verification, make sure the name on that ID aligns with either your legal current name or the name the receiving agency expects.

Security advice
- Avoid emailing your full SSN. If you must provide it, use their secure portal or deliver it in person.
- Prefer sharing only last four digits when asked for identification unless full SSN is specifically required and the channel is secure.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short, secure message to the agency asking whether they require the exact SSA name (I won’t include your SSN unless you expressly instruct me and confirm a secure sending method).
- Find the SSA instructions/requirements for changing your name.
- Help you word the name exactly as it appears on a specific document (you can paste the name without the SSN).

Which form or organization is this for? That will let me give tailored guidance.

