Short answer: yes — for most official, government, payroll, tax, and benefits forms your name should match exactly the name on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Mismatches commonly cause processing delays, tax/W‑2 problems, identity-verification failures, or requests for additional documentation.

What to know and do
- Why exact match matters: agencies and employers match your name + SSN against SSA records. Differences (missing middle name/initial, different order, hyphens, nicknames, maiden vs. married name, small spelling differences) can cause rejects or manual review.
- When variations are sometimes OK: informal or marketing forms often accept common variants (e.g., Dave vs. David). Banks or service providers may accept a different name if you supply ID or proof. But don’t assume — check the form’s instructions or ask the requester.
- If the form specifically says “name as shown on Social Security card” — use the SSA name exactly.
- If your name on the form doesn’t match your SSA record, you can:
  - Use the exact name from your Social Security card on the form (preferred).
  - Add an “also known as” / AKA field if the form has it.
  - Attach or be ready to provide supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license) or a signed affidavit if the agency accepts that.
  - Contact the requester (HR, agency, bank) and ask what they accept — they may allow a proof-of-name-change document rather than an immediate SSA update.
- If you recently changed your name (marriage, divorce, court order) you may need to update SSA records (form SS-5). Required items and timing:
  - SSA typically requires proof of identity, proof of U.S. citizenship or lawful status (if applicable), and the legal document showing the name change.
  - Processing can take a few weeks; plan ahead if the form is time-sensitive.

Security note
- Avoid sending your full SSN in email or unsecured messages. Use secure upload portals, encrypted attachments, or provide only the last four digits when the recipient accepts that for verification. If they insist on a full SSN, confirm they have a secure method.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to HR/agency asking whether the SSA-name must match and what proofs they accept.
- Help you find specific SSA instructions or the SS-5 form requirements.
- Review the exact wording on the form (paste the form instructions or the relevant line) and suggest the best response.

