Short answer: for any official or identity-verification purpose, use the exact legal name that appears on your Social Security record (the name on your Social Security card). Small informal differences (nicknames, omitted middle name/initial) sometimes pass, but mismatches are a common cause of processing delays or rejections — so match the SSA record and include suffixes (Jr., III) and the same spelling/punctuation when possible.

Practical guidance
- Check the form’s instructions first — some forms explicitly say “as shown on Social Security card” or allow “first and last name only.”
- If the form is for taxes, payroll, government benefits, banking, or proving identity, match your SSA record exactly (including middle name/initial and suffix).
- Hyphens, apostrophes, and diacritics: some systems normalize them, others do not. If your Social Security card includes them, try to include them; if the online form won’t accept them, contact the organization to confirm how they want it entered.
- If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, court order), make sure the SSA has your updated name before submitting documents that will be matched against SSN records.
- If the organization asks only for the last four digits of your SSN (common for identity checks), that’s generally fine — avoid sending the full SSN unless you’re on a secure, trusted channel.

If you’re worried about a specific form: tell me what type of form it is (tax, bank, DMV, employer onboarding, passport application, etc.) and I can give targeted advice or draft a short message you can send to the organization asking whether an exact match is required. Also, please avoid posting your full SSN here — only share non-sensitive details or ask me to help draft a message you can send securely.

