Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — use your full legal name as it appears on your Social Security records (and on the ID you’ll present). Mismatches can delay processing or trigger verification requests, so it’s best to match exactly when the form asks for your legal name.

Details and practical steps
- What “match exactly” means
  - Agencies and employers expect the name that appears on your Social Security record (first, middle, last, and any suffix like Jr./III) or the government ID you will present.
  - Minor differences in punctuation, spaces, or use of a middle initial vs. full middle name are sometimes tolerated, but legal-name differences (maiden vs. married name, omitted middle name when it’s on the SSN record, different last name spelling) often cause problems.
- When exact matching is most important
  - Tax forms, payroll/W‑2, Social Security matters, passport/visa, and legal documents generally require the legal name on record.
  - For casual forms (newsletters, internal sign-ups) exact legal matching is less critical.
- If your name on the form does not match your SSN record
  - Expect a verification request or processing delay.
  - You may need to provide supporting documents (SSN card, marriage certificate, court order) or update your Social Security record with the SSA.
- If you changed your name
  - Either use the name currently on your SSN record, or update the SSA first (visit SSA office or use SSA instructions) and then submit the form with the updated name.
- Formatting (hyphens, punctuation, middle initial)
  - When in doubt, use the same spelling and sequence of names as on your SSN card. Hyphens and punctuation usually aren’t the core issue — the legal name itself is.
- Privacy and security
  - Do not share your full SSN in emails or public messages. Only provide it to trusted parties over secure channels when absolutely necessary.
  - If you want, I can help you draft a secure message to the agency asking how they want names formatted without including your SSN.

If you tell me what type of form it is (tax, employer, government benefit, bank, etc.) I can give more specific wording to use or draft a short query you can send to the organization.

