I won’t repeat your SSN here. A few clear points and practical steps:

1) When an exact match is required
- Government and tax forms: If the form ties to Social Security Administration (SSA) or IRS records (e.g., I‑9, SSA correspondence, W‑2/W‑4, tax returns, benefits), use the name that exactly matches your Social Security card. Mismatches can cause verification failures, delays, or incorrect tax reporting.
- ID/identity documents: Passports, driver’s licenses, and bank identity checks generally require the same legal name shown on the ID you present.
- Legal name changes and suffixes: Include suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) and the exact spelling used on your legal documents.

2) When small differences are often acceptable
- Non-governmental or informal forms (some employer HR forms, internal questionnaires, event registrations) sometimes accept commonly used variations (use of a middle initial, nicknames, hyphenation). But acceptance varies — check the form’s instructions.

3) What to do if the name on the form doesn’t match your SSA record
- If you expect this will be a problem, either:
  - Update the name on your SSA record (if your legal name has changed). This requires providing legal documentation to SSA.
