Short answer: yes — when a form asks for your legal name (or says “name as shown on Social Security card,” “legal name,” or similar) you should use the exact legal spelling that appears on your Social Security card or other government ID. Small variations (middle initial present/absent, punctuation, diacritics, abbreviations) sometimes are accepted for informal or internal forms, but mismatches can cause delays, verification failures, or requests for additional documentation.

Practical guidance
- If the form explicitly says “legal name” or “name as shown on Social Security card,” enter the name exactly as on your Social Security card.  
- If you changed your name (marriage, court order, etc.), write your current legal name and, if there’s a field for “previous name,” include the former name or use “also known as” / “formerly known as” if the form allows.  
- If the form is ambiguous, contact the requester (HR, bank, government agency) and ask whether small differences (middle initial, periods, accents) are acceptable. That avoids rework.  
- If a mismatch is likely to affect benefits, payroll, taxes, or legal records, insist on an exact match and, if necessary, provide supporting documents (Social Security card, marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license).

If you need to correct your official name with SSA (Social Security Administration)
- You’ll usually need proof of identity (driver’s license, passport) and proof of the name change (marriage certificate, divorce decree, or court order).  
- Check SSA’s website or call your local SSA office for current instructions and required documents.

Security note about SSNs
- Don’t email or post your full SSN. Only provide the full number over secure channels or in person when absolutely required. Sharing just the last four digits is common for routine identity checks, but it’s not a substitute for the official SSN when an agency explicitly requires the whole number.  
- If a form asks for SSN, prefer submitting it through a secure portal, by phone to a verified number, or in person rather than plain email.

If you want, I can:
- Review the exact wording of the form (redact any SSN or other sensitive numbers) and suggest what to enter or what note to add; or  
- Draft a short message you can send to the form requester asking whether the exact spelling is required.

Not legal advice — if this is for a court/legal filing, consider confirming with counsel or the issuing agency.

