Short answer: in most official contexts that use your SSN, you should use your full legal name exactly as it appears on your Social Security record and government ID. Small differences (middle initial vs full middle name, punctuation, or common abbreviations) are sometimes accepted, but mismatches can cause delays or verification failures.

Details and practical steps

- Why exactness matters
  - Agencies (SSA, IRS) and many financial institutions match the name you give to their records when verifying your SSN. A mismatch can lead to rejected forms, delayed tax refunds, payroll/benefits problems, or extra identity verification steps.
  - For tax filings, the IRS expects the name on your return to match SSA records; differences can trigger processing delays.
  - For identity documents (I-9, passport, driver’s license), you must present the name exactly as shown on the supporting documents.

- What differences are usually okay
  - Minor punctuation, capitalization, or use/omission of a middle initial vs middle name is often tolerated.
  - Nicknames or shortened names (e.g., “Mike” vs “Michael”) are not acceptable where the form specifically requires your legal name.
  - If the form has a “former name,” “AKA,” or “maiden name” field, include the other name there in addition to your legal name.

- If your name on the form doesn’t match your Social Security record
  - If the discrepancy is minor, ask the receiving organization whether they will accept it or request documentation.
  - If the name on SSA records is wrong or out of date, correct it with the Social Security Administration (SSA). You generally need to complete Form SS-5 and provide original or certified documents proving identity, citizenship/immigration status, and the legal name change (e.g., marriage certificate, court order). Visit ssa.gov or your local SSA office for specifics and required documents.
  - For employers or banks, bring your SSA card or other primary ID (driver’s license, passport) and any name-change documents to resolve the mismatch.

- What to do right now
  - Check the form’s instructions: some forms explicitly say “use your legal name as shown on your Social Security card.”
  - If the form offers an “also known as” or “previous name” field, use it.
  - If unsure, contact the organization (HR, bank, agency) before submitting to avoid delays.
  - Avoid sharing your full SSN in insecure channels; only provide it where required and on secure forms.

If you want, I can:
- Look up the specific form’s instructions if you tell me which form it is (e.g., tax return, I-9, bank account application).
- Draft exactly how to write your name on the form (legal name plus former name line).
- Walk you through how to update your name with SSA (list of documents and next steps).

